We need to create them (already created maybe earlier). We'll need to allocate vehicle, attach profile, confirm prepared, assign operator, apply operational constraint, confirm operator alignment, assign compliance certificate, verify compliance, finalize (dispatch_ready), allocate dispatch slot, execute schedule dispatch.

We have two schedule candidates; we can process them sequentially.

We have one operator resource; we need to assign to each, then release after use.

We have one compliance certificate; need to assign, verify, finalize, then release before next.

We have one route task profile; need to attach/detach each time.

We have two operational constraints: ocα for sa, ocβ for sb.

We have two vehicles; we can allocate va to sa, then after dispatch release, allocate vb to sb, etc.

We have one dispatch slot; reuse.

Thus we need to do for each schedule candidate:

- create_candidate (if not already)
- allocate_vehicle_unit (choose vehicle)
- attach_task_profile
- confirm_candidate_preparedness
- assign_operator_resource
- apply_operational_constraint (using appropriate oc)
- confirm_operator_alignment
- assign_compliance_certificate
- verify_compliance_certificate
- finalize_candidate_after_compliance_validation (gives dispatch_ready)
- allocate_dispatch_slot_for_candidate (requires dispatch_ready, candidate_task_profile_linked, dispatch_slot_available)
- execute_schedule_dispatch (requires dispatch_allocated, candidate_vehicle_linked, candidate_dispatch_slot_linked)
- detach_task_profile (optional)
- release_operator_resource
- release_compliance_certificate
- (vehicle becomes available after dispatch)

We also need to release operational constraint? Not needed; it's consumed by apply_operational_constraint (makes it unavailable). After we finish, we may not need it again. Since we have separate oc for each candidate, we consume them.

Thus we need to have ocα and ocβ available initially; they are.

